End to end: 10 concurrent cold creates, same box, same S3, same VMM

experiments/metal-cluster-20260726/concurrent-start.sh with CATS_PHASES=create,
10 Cats, 2 GiB root disks from the same 512 MiB golden rootfs, vhost-user block
path. The only difference between the two rows is the build: `copy` is this
branch's parent (delta packs, no golden root), which streams the rootfs into each
volume; `reference` is this branch.

  build                       wall ms   min ms   p50 ms   max ms  failed ops
  copy the rootfs (parent)     121448    11775    88477   121448           4
  reference a shared image      61637     8203    28134    61634           0

* The copy row reproduces the 128 s recorded on 2026-07-26 for the same shape, on
  a different box on a different day, which is a useful check on both numbers.
* Four of ten create operations failed on the copy path and none on the reference
  path: uploading 512 MiB per Cat ten times over is not only slow, it pushes the
  launch past its own timeouts.
* Single create on this branch: 6.9 s for the first Cat on the node (which
  publishes the image) and 4.5 s for the second. The Guest boots off referenced
  chunks -- `/dev/vda 2.0G 279M used`, 411 binaries in /usr/bin, real ext4.

The remaining 61.6 s is not storage. Storage for all ten creates is 167 ms
(measured above); the wall clock is a ~6 s-per-create ramp (min 8.2 s, p50 28 s,
max 61.6 s) that looks fully serialized end to end. The kernel-nbd path gives
essentially the same number (60.4 s), so it is not the block path either --
`launch_allocates_node_global_state` only takes the node-wide lock for kernel-nbd,
and switching it off changed nothing. Whatever serializes cold create now lives
above the node agent's launch path, and it is the next thing to measure: golden
roots removed the storage cost of a concurrent create, and what is left is
somebody else's queue.

One Guest in an earlier 10-way vhost-user run failed with "guest never became
healthy over vsock" (9/10 running). It did not recur in the run recorded above
(10/10, 0 failures), so it is logged here as seen-once rather than diagnosed.

